"Requirements for the code implementation:\n- Data loading: Read the u.data file as whitespace-delimited with 4 columns: user_id, item_id, rating, timestamp; parse as integers; handle minor formatting gracefully or fail with a clear error.\n- Unique counts: Compute total number of unique user_ids and total number of unique item_ids.\n- Top users: Identify the top 3 most active users by rating count (descending). Break ties deterministically by ascending user_id. Output a list of (user_id, rating_count).\n- Top user mean rating: For the #1 most active user, calculate their mean rating across all ratings.\n- Co-occurrence: For Item 50 and Item 181, calculate the number of users who rated both items (intersection of user_id sets). If either item is not present, co-occurrence = 0.\n- Timestamp validation: Convert timestamps to years; consider valid only years 1997 and 1998 (i.e., 1997-01-01 through 1998-12-31). Count how many records have a year outside this set. Return the count and, if helpful, sample offending lines.\n- Output schema: Provide total_users (int), total_items (int), top3_users (list of pairs), mean_rating_top1 (float), cooccurrence_50_181 (int), out_of_range_timestamp_count (int).\n- Edge cases: If item 50 or 181 is missing, cooccurrence = 0. If lines are malformed, raise a clear error.\n- Implementation constraints: Use efficient, vectorized operations (e.g., pandas) to handle ~100k rows; ensure deterministic results by fixed sorting rules; no external dependencies beyond standard data libraries; run in a single script; provide clear error messages.\n- Reproducibility and robustness: Document and implement deterministic tie-breaking and robust handling of missing values.\n- Interface: Provide a simple function accepting a file path and returning a structured summary with the metrics.\n"